  "Number": 26,
  "Question": "Find the set of \\(x\\)-values satisfying the inequality \\[ \\left|\\frac{5-x}{3}\\right|\\lt2. \\] [The symbol \\(|a|\\) means \\(+a\\) if \\(a\\) is positive, \\(-a\\) if \\(a\\) is negative, \\(0\\) if \\(a\\) is zero. The notation \\(1\\lt a\\lt2\\) means that \\(a\\) can have any value between \\(1\\) and \\(2,\\) excluding \\(1\\) and \\(2.\\)]",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(1\\lt x\\lt11\\)",
    "B": "\\(-1\\lt x\\lt11\\)",
    "C": "\\(x\\lt11\\)",
    "D": "\\(x\\gt11\\)",
    "E": "\\(|x|\\lt6\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "B",
  "Solution": "An absolute value less than \\(2\\) means that the expression inside lies strictly between \\(-2\\) and \\(2\\). Since the denominator \\(3\\) is positive, multiplying by it preserves both inequality signs:\n\\[\n-2<\\frac{5-x}{3}<2\n\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\n-6<5-x<6.\n\\]\nSubtract \\(5\\), then multiply by \\(-1\\), reversing the inequality signs:\n\\[\n-11<-x<1\n\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\n-1<x<11.\n\\]\nAt either endpoint the original absolute value equals \\(2\\), so neither endpoint is included. The answer is \\(\\boxed{-1<x<11}\\), choice \\(\\mathrm{B}\\).",
